From 128b399f43a999cbd455bc72bdd7daef56b3d2a8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 10:05:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] PS4: idle cores and service threads spin before sleeping,
 on-time timers, faster ARM decoding, and where a frame's time goes

A console's Mario Kart 8 Deluxe ran its races at 12 fps with the PS4's GPU
2-5% busy and no thread busy all the time: the emulated cores slept 26-45%
of the time in condition variable waits, between handoffs.

- PhysicalCore::Idle spins up to 50 us before it sleeps (m_is_interrupted
  atomic): work handed to an idle core (a thread another core made ready, a
  service's reply) usually comes within that, and waking a sleeping host
  thread costs more on the PS4.
- Host threads waiting for the game (the services on host threads: nvdrv,
  vi, audio, FS) spin up to 50 us too before sleeping (KThread's dummy
  thread wait; m_dummy_thread_runnable atomic): a service call wakes one.
- The timing thread learns how much its timed waits overshoot and waits
  that much less (at most 1 ms), spinning the rest, so the game's timers,
  vsync and audio fire on time (a new event ends the spin).
- DecodeVFP and DecodeASIMD reject encodings outside their spaces with one
  test before trying their ~60 and ~150 matchers (every pattern checked):
  every ordinary ARM instruction went through both lists first, 10-15% of
  an emulated core whenever code is translated.
- common/ps4_perf.h counts, for the launcher's 30-second line: idle cores
  woken while spinning or after sleeping (and that wake-up's delay), the
  game's service calls and their round trips, timer events' lateness and the
  learned overshoot, blocks translated and cache flushes, and memory accesses
  that left the inline page table walk (per core).
